[
    {
        "content": "<p>Can someone explain to me how does the following proof work:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Category</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ObjectProperty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Abelian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ContainsZero</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderKernels</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderCokernels</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderFiniteProducts</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Abelian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">FullSubcategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I don't know how to track the logical chain here, because there is no goal displayed in InfoView at all so that the reasoning is invisible to me.</p>",
        "id": 609470608,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1783693733
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Which bit specifically are you asking about? I guess a pedantic answer to your question is that the definition of <code>Abelian</code> in Lean is</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Abelian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">extends</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Preadditive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsNormalMonoCategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsNormalEpiCategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">has_finite_products</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasFiniteProducts</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">has_kernels</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasKernels</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">has_cokernels</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasCokernels</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>so you are asking how typeclass inference is solving <code>HasFiniteProducts (P.FullSubcategory)</code>, <code>HasKernels (P.FullSubcategory)</code> and <code>HasCokernels (P.FullSubcategory)</code>. And you can just ask Lean this, e.g.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Limits</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Category</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ObjectProperty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Abelian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ContainsZero</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderKernels</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderCokernels</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderFiniteProducts</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">synth</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasFiniteProducts</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">FullSubcategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- ObjectProperty.instHasFiniteProductsFullSubcategoryOfIsClosedUnderFiniteProducts P</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 609472928,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1783694342
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Aha, I see. So basically I have to #synth <code>Preadditive/IsNormalMonoCategory/IsNormalEpiCategory/HasFiniteProducts/HasKernels/HasCokernels</code> one by one, and perhaps further #synth the constructors involving in the above?<br>\nEdit: do we have a button like \"Go to (1st order?)Typeclass inference\" similar to \"Go to Definition\"?</p>",
        "id": 609476454,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1783695219
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can use</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Abelian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ContainsZero</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderKernels</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderCokernels</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderFiniteProducts</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Abelian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">FullSubcategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>to get a goal state. The proof is presumably <code>constructor</code></p>",
        "id": 609477463,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1783695479
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110038\">@Kevin Buzzard</span> Another point I was asking about is that why Lean solves it automatically? There is a tactic <code>infer_instance</code>. In the above example, we don't even need to make this command. What is the meaning of <code>infer_instance</code> then?</p>",
        "id": 609483628,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1783697079
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe internally <code>[has_finite_products : HasFiniteProducts C]</code> is the same as <code>has_finite_products : HasFiniteProducts C := inferInstance</code>, i.e. a field of a structure with default value?</p>",
        "id": 609486109,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1783697806
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1097608\">Jiaxi Mo</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Magic.20empty.20proof/near/609483628\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>In the above example, we don't even need to make this command. What is the meaning of <code>infer_instance</code> then?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Lean can (try to) infer automatically instance implicit arguments, but when you are in tactic mode, lean is simply not constantly checking if your goal is an <code>instance</code>. <code>infer_instance</code> essentially serves to just tell it that, and then triggers the same search algorithm. Your proof seems empty because <code>where</code> is syntactic sugar that just expands to <code>{ ... }</code> notation. Perhaps writing it this way can make it seem less magical:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Abelian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ContainsZero</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderKernels</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderCokernels</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsClosedUnderFiniteProducts</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Abelian</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">FullSubcategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{}</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 609490638,
        "sender_full_name": "Luigi Massacci",
        "timestamp": 1783699148
    }
]